The radius of the circle with center $R$ is decreased so that

$\bullet$ the circle with center $R$ remains tangent to $BC$,

$\bullet$ the circle with center $R$ remains tangent to the other three circles, and

$\bullet$ the circle with center $P$ becomes tangent to the other three circles.

The radii and tangencies of the other three circles stay the same. This changes the size and shape of $\triangle ABC$. $r$ is the new radius of the circle with center $R$. $r$ is of the form $\frac{a+\sqrt{b}}{c}$. Find $a+b+c$.

results.gold_solution:
After the described transformation, we obtain the following diagram. [asy]
size(250);
pair A, B, C, P, Q, R, S;
int x;
x=4;
B=(-.45,0);
Q=(2.414,1);
R=(x,.618);
C=(2x+.45,0);
S=(2x-2.414,1);
P=(x,2.236);
A=(x,3.55);
draw(A--B--C--A);
draw(circle(Q,1));
draw(circle(S,1));
draw(circle(R,.618));
draw(circle(P,1));
label("A", A, N);
label("B", B, SW);
label("C", C, SE);
label("R", R, dir(270));
label("Q", Q, SW);
label("S", S, SE);
label("P", P, N);
dot(Q);
dot(P);
dot(S);
dot(R);
[/asy]

Drop perpendiculars from $Q$, $R$ and $S$ to $D$, $E$ and $F$ respectively on $BC$.


[asy]
size(250);
pair P, Q, R, S, B, C, D, E, F, Y;
P=(4,2.236);
Q=(2.414,1);
R=(4,.618);
S=(5.586,1);
B=(0,0);
C=(8,0);
D=(2.414,0);
E=(4,0);
F=(5.586,0);
Y=(4,1);
draw(circle(P,1));
draw(circle(Q,1));
draw(circle(S,1));
draw(circle(R,.618));
draw(B--C);
draw(Q--D);
draw(P--E);
draw(F--S);
draw(Q--S);
draw(Q--P--S--R--Q);
label("D", D, dir(270));
label("E", E, dir(270));
label("F", F, dir(270));
label("P", P, N);
label("Q", Q, NW);
label("S", S, NE);
label("R", R, SW);
label("Y", Y, NW);
dot(P);
dot(Q);
dot(R);
dot(S);
[/asy]

Since the circles with centers $Q$, $R$ and $S$ are tangent to $BC$, then $D$, $E$ and $F$ are the points of tangency of these circles to $BC$. Thus, $QD=SF=1$ and $RE=r$.

Join $QR$, $RS$, $PS$, $PQ$, and $PR$. Since we are connecting centers of tangent circles, $PQ=PS=2$ and $QR=RS=PR=1+r$.

Join $QS$. By symmetry, $PRE$ is a straight line (that is, $PE$ passes through $R$). Since $QS$ is parallel to $BC$, $QS$ is perpendicular to $PR$, meeting at $Y$.

Since $QD=1$, $YE=1$.  Since $RE=r$, $YR=1-r$. Since $QR=1+r$, $YR=1-r$ and $\triangle QYR$ is right-angled at $Y$, then by the Pythagorean Theorem,
\begin{align*}
QY^2 &= QR^2 - YR^2\\
&= (1+r)^2 - (1-r)^2 \\
&= (1+2r+r^2)-(1-2r+r^2) \\
&= 4r.
\end{align*}Since $PR=1+r$ and $YR=1-r$, then $PY = PR-YR=2r$. Since $\triangle PYQ$ is right-angled at $Y$,
\begin{align*}
PY^2 + YQ^2 & = PQ^2 \\
(2r)^2 + 4r & = 2^2 \\
4r^2 + 4r & =4 \\
r^2 + r - 1 & = 0.
\end{align*}By the quadratic formula, $r = \dfrac{-1\pm \sqrt{1^2-4(1)(-1)}}{2} = \dfrac{-1\pm\sqrt{5}}{2}$.
Since $r>0$, then $r = \dfrac{-1+\sqrt{5}}{2}$ (which is the reciprocal of the famous ``golden ratio"). Thus, $a+b+c=-1+5+2=\boxed{6}$.

results.problem:
$ABCDEFGH$ shown below is a right rectangular prism.  If the volume of pyramid $ABCH$ is 20, then what is the volume of $ABCDEFGH$?

[asy]

import three;

triple A,B,C,D,EE,F,G,H;

A = (0,0,0);

B = (5,0,0);

C = (5,6,0);

D= (0,6,0);

EE = (0,0,4);

F = B+EE;

G = C + EE;

H = D + EE;

draw(B--C--D);

draw(B--A--D,dashed);

draw(EE--F--G--H--EE);

draw(A--EE,dashed);

draw(B--F);

draw(C--G);

draw(D--H);

label("$A$",A,S);

label("$B$",B,W);

label("$C$",C,S);

label("$D$",D,E);

label("$E$",EE,N);

label("$F$",F,W);

label("$G$",G,SW);

label("$H$",H,E);

[/asy]

results.gold_solution:
We add the edges of the pyramid to our diagram below.

[asy]
import three;
triple A,B,C,D,EE,F,G,H;
A = (0,0,0);
B = (5,0,0);
C = (5,6,0);
D= (0,6,0);
EE = (0,0,4);
F = B+EE;
G = C + EE;
H = D + EE;
draw(B--C--D);
draw(B--A--D,dashed);
draw(EE--F--G--H--EE);
draw(B--H--A--EE,dashed);
draw(A--C,dashed);
draw(B--F);
draw(C--G);
draw(D--H--C);
label("$A$",A,SSW);
label("$B$",B,W);
label("$C$",C,S);
label("$D$",D,E);
label("$E$",EE,N);
label("$F$",F,W);
label("$G$",G,SW);
label("$H$",H,E);
[/asy]

Taking $ABC$ to be the base of pyramid $ABCH$, the height is $HD$.  Therefore, the volume of pyramid $ABCH$ is $$\frac{[ABC](HD)}{3}.$$We are given that this volume equals 20, so we have \[{[ABC]\cdot HD}=60.\]The volume of prism $ABCDEFGH$ is the product of area of $ABCD$ and the height of the prism, which equals $HD$.   The area of $ABC$ is half the area of rectangle $ABCD$, so we have  \begin{align*}
\text{Volume of }ABCDEFGH &= ([ABCD])(HD) \\
&= 2([ABC])(HD) \\
&= \boxed{120}.\end{align*}

